143:
"Avyāpajjhārāmā viharatha" means: dwell greatly delighting in dwelling with loving-kindness and dwelling with compassion toward all beings. By this, he speaks of distancing oneself from ill-will and the defilements that reside in the same place as ill-will. In the phrase 'tesaṃ vo', 'vo' is merely a particle. "Pavivekārāmā viharatha" means: dwell making all forms of seclusion—such as physical seclusion (kāya-viveka) and seclusion by substitution of opposites (tadaṅga-viveka)—your place of delight. Just as the words beginning with 'imāya mayaṃ' show the manner in which the thought of safety (khema-vitakka) arises for them, the words beginning with 'kiṃ akusalaṃ' show the manner in which the thought of seclusion (paviveka-vitakka) arises. In that passage, just as a seeker of the wholesome who wishes to fulfill blameless states must search for wholesome states, one who wishes to abandon blameworthy states must also search for (examine) the unwholesome; thus he said "kiṃ akusalaṃ" (what is unwholesome?) and so forth. This is because higher knowledge (abhiññā) is the precursor to full understanding (pariññā), abandoning (pahāna), realization (sacchikiriyā), and development (bhāvanā).

In that passage, "kiṃ akusalaṃ" means: what is the unwholesome? What is its characteristic by nature? Or what are its function (rasa), manifestation (paccupaṭṭhāna), and proximate cause (padaṭṭhāna)? Thus he shows the method of reviewing the unwholesome through its nature, function, etc. This thought occurs in the sense of a beginner (ādikammika). These two phrases, "kiṃ appahīnaṃ" and "kiṃ pajahāma", occur in the sense of those in training (sekha). Therefore, "kiṃ appahīnaṃ" means: among the unwholesome states such as the fetter of sensual lust, which unwholesome state has not yet been completely cut off by our path? "Kiṃ pajahāma" means: which unwholesome state should be completely destroyed? Alternatively, "kiṃ pajahāma" means: among transgression (vītikkama), obsession (pariyuṭṭhāna), and underlying tendency (anusaya), what group of unwholesome states should we now abandon? Some teachers read it only as "kiṃ appahīnaṃ". According to their view, it means: among the many varieties of unwholesome states such as the fetter of wrong view, which unwholesome state, in what manner, or by which path, have we not yet abandoned? The remaining passages are to be understood in the same manner as previously stated.

In the verses, "Buddhaṃ" means: because there is no knowable thing (ñeyya-dhamma) outside of the Four Noble Truths, and because he has penetrated and known the Four Noble Truths without distortion through his self-evolved knowledge (sayambhū-ñāṇa), he is called 'Buddha'. Thus it has been said:
"What should be known through higher knowledge has been known by me;
What should be developed has been developed;
What should be abandoned has been abandoned by me;
Therefore, O Brahmin, I am a Buddha."
Excluding the Great Bodhisattva, because he endured and carried the entire requisites of enlightenment (bodhi-sambhāra) and the great compassion which others are unable to endure or carry; and because he endured and crushed the five Maras whom others cannot overcome; and because he carried out the Buddha's work—which others cannot carry out—namely, disciplining veneya beings according to their needs by knowing their underlying tendencies (anusaya) and so forth, for their welfare in this life and the next; or because he is one who habitually performs such tasks well...
